WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:03.269
Thanks to Ground News for helping support this episode.

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:06.973
You are traveling at the speed of light right now,

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:09.075
though it might not seem like it.

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.878
Maybe you're sitting in a chair thinking you're at rest.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:14.147
At the fastest, you're on a plane.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:16.616
That isn’t the speed of light? Is it?

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:19.586
The problem is you're not thinking four dimensionally.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:22.455
You're only thinking of your speed through space,

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:25.158
when you also have a speed through time.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:28.862
If we consider them both together as a spacetime speed,

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:33.466
then the speed of all matter, including you and me, is the speed of light.

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:36.970
[Intro Music]

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:41.307
Hey Crazies. What we're talking about is something called 4-velocity,

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:44.511
which is just short for four-dimensional velocity.

00:00:44.511 --> 00:00:46.546
But to understand what that is,

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.848
we're going to need to start in three dimensions.

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:52.419
Regular old velocity, also known as 3-velocity,

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:55.855
is a way to represent two things simultaneously:

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:57.991
How fast something is moving

00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:59.926
and in what direction it's moving.

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:04.664
That's why we represent it with an arrow. It's the speed and direction.

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:05.799
It's pretty straightforward

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:08.535
and it's a very useful way to look at velocity,

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:11.771
if you never intend on going beyond three dimensions.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.507
For that we need to think like a mathematician.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:17.377
We're going to generalize things to death.

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:23.249
First, if we draw the path, we can see the velocity is always tangent to that path.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.852
Is that how you go off on a random train of thought sometimes?

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:28.888
Actually, that tangent uses the word metaphorically.

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:31.758
We're using the older, more literal definition.

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:36.463
Tangent comes from the old Latin word “tangere” meaning “to touch.”

00:01:36.463 --> 00:01:41.234
In our case, velocity is always along something called a tangent line.

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:45.405
That's an imaginary line touching our path without crossing it

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:49.843
The velocity is along the tangent line at every point on the path.

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:54.147
It's something universally true of all real velocities is everywhere.

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:56.182
But that's just the direction though, right?

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:57.484
I mean, what about the speed?

00:01:57.484 --> 00:02:01.588
Speed is the length of the arrow, which is determined by another rule.

00:02:01.588 --> 00:02:03.690
It's usually measured in miles per hour,

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.360
or kilometers per hour, or meters per second, something like that.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.530
A unit of space divided by a unit of time.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.098
And that's not a coincidence.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:15.835
Speed is defined as the change in location

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:18.938
divided by the amount of time that change took

00:02:18.938 --> 00:02:22.442
In rigorous mathematical terms, that would be written like this:

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:25.545
X stands for location, T is time,

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:28.515
and each of these Ds just means a tiny change.

00:02:28.515 --> 00:02:33.419
So, this is a tiny change in location divided by a tiny passage of time.

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:36.923
If the change in location is larger in the same amount of time,

00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:38.758
then the object is going faster.

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:41.561
Visually, we represent that with a longer arrow.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:44.397
So far, this is all a bit geometric though.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:46.232
We need something better for physics.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:51.905
Thankfully, all the way back in 1637, Rene Descartes invented the coordinate system,

00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:55.542
a mathematical tool that turns geometry into algebra.

00:02:55.542 --> 00:03:00.113
It's such a powerful invention that we named the most basic type after him.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:03.316
They're called Cartesian coordinates for his Latinized name:

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:05.185
Renatus Cartesius.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:10.890
Anyway, coordinates allow us to write things like velocity as an array of numbers.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.693
You just draw a few axes, one for each dimension,

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:17.830
and then project your velocity arrow onto each of those special axes.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:22.235
Those three values are unique to that specific speed and direction.

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:25.638
If the velocity changes, then those numbers change accordingly.

00:03:25.638 --> 00:03:28.508
Okay, sure, but how does this apply to 4-velocity?

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:32.512
Time is just a dimension, so it gets an axis too.

00:03:32.512 --> 00:03:36.049
We're used to thinking of time as uniquely different from space.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:40.086
It's measured using clocks, whereas space is measured with rulers.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:44.490
But, over a century ago, we learned that space and time are very similar.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.893
Not exactly the same, but similar.

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:51.831
So similar, in fact, that we can visualize time the same way we do space:

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:54.200
as a coordinate on an axis.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.705
As time passes, everything is just moving forward on the time axis.

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:02.308
Think of it like the perspective of some omniscient deity or something,

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.043
but in our imaginations.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.379
In this four-dimensional spacetime,

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:10.149
a path shows us everywhere an object will be and when.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:14.787
We can see everything it will ever do, within our ability to physically predict.

00:04:14.787 --> 00:04:18.324
That's why every point on a path like this is called an “event.”

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:21.027
If we draw tangent lines at each of those events,

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:24.964
we know the 4-velocity will be along those lines toward the future,

00:04:24.964 --> 00:04:26.899
because that's what velocities do.

00:04:26.899 --> 00:04:29.702
Why are the lengths of all those arrows the same though?

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:32.171
Because of a weird quirk of reality.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:35.241
Say there's a spacewalking astronaut and a rock.

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:40.380
Since we see the astronaut as stationary, we know we're using the astronaut’s coordinates.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.718
Her coordinates also include a clock, which measures coordinate time in this picture.

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:50.556
But the rock has its own clock, which measures its own passage of time.

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:51.891
It's proper time.

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:57.230
And, if that rock is moving relative to the astronaut, then its clock will tick at a different rate.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:02.402
This is the 4-velocity of the rock written in the astronauts coordinates.

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:03.836
This is the spatial speed,

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:07.106
which is why it looks a lot like the three-dimensional speed from earlier.

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:10.910
This other one is the temporal speed, the speed through time.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.546
Just like before, this X still means location,

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:16.616
but more specifically the coordinate location.

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:18.951
As in the location measured by the astronaut,

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:21.354
because that's who the coordinates belong to.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:26.592
The T is time measured by the astronaut, also known as coordinate time.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:30.163
This weird looking T on the bottom is actually a Greek tao,

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:33.099
which represents proper or personal time,

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:35.335
as in the time measured by the rock.

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:39.205
When we're working with four-dimensional spacetime, we're working with relativity.

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:43.476
Things like time, space, and velocity are all relative to the observer.

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:46.379
That's why we need two different times in these equations.

00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:49.182
One is for the rock and one is for the astronaut.

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:51.818
Why aren’t there two sets of space coordinates?

00:05:51.818 --> 00:05:55.254
Because the rock doesn't move in space, according to the rock.

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:58.758
From the rocks perspective, the astronaut is moving instead.

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:01.027
But that's not the perspective we were looking from.

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:03.563
Our coordinates belong to the astronaut.

00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:06.566
Time passes for both the rock and the astronaut,

00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:09.902
but the rock only changes location for the astronaut.

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:12.805
The bottom tells us which object we're looking at.

00:06:12.805 --> 00:06:14.974
The top tells us who was looking.

00:06:14.974 --> 00:06:17.343
From there, it's just a few more lines of math.

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:19.412
I go through them in my book, if you're interested.

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:23.015
But I think I can give you a more intuitive understanding in this video.

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:27.954
Say we were to graph the rocks proper time against its coordinate location.

00:06:27.954 --> 00:06:32.058
That's time measured by the rock and space measured by the astronaut.

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:34.360
This is super unconventional.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.397
I think it'll help in this case, but I would not make a habit out of it.

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:42.268
Had the rock been sitting still, its motion would be directed along the time axis only.

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:44.570
It would only be traveling through time.

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.406
But she sees the rock moving.

00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:50.143
You might think the arrow would stretch to include a change in location.

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:51.778
That's not what happens though.

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:57.617
In relativity, the faster we see something moving, the slower we see its personal time pass.

00:06:57.617 --> 00:07:00.720
The arrow doesn't stretch. It just turns.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.092
Any gain in spatial speed is matched by a loss in temporal speed.

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:09.095
No matter how fast an object is going in space,

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:13.599
the coordinates conspire to keep the tip of the arrow on this circle

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:16.436
and, therefore, the length of the arrow the same.

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:18.671
That length is the speed of light.

00:07:18.671 --> 00:07:24.277
Well, technically, it's the imaginary “i” multiplied by the speed of light, but close enough.

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:25.812
There's just one problem.

00:07:25.812 --> 00:07:30.316
This graphic only works for spatial speeds less than the speed of light.

00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:33.119
At the speed of light, the whole thing breaks down.

00:07:33.186 --> 00:07:34.687
Remember when I said this?

00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:36.522
This is super unconventional.

00:07:36.522 --> 00:07:39.992
I think it'll help in this case, but I would not make a habit out of it.

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:43.629
Photons move at exactly the speed of light through space.

00:07:43.763 --> 00:07:44.664
Fast Fast!

00:07:44.664 --> 00:07:47.533
By this reasoning, it's lost all of its time.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:51.838
Photons have no proper time. They have no... anything.

00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:54.540
For photons, nothing exists.

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:58.311
That's why we call their paths through spacetime “null paths.”

00:07:58.311 --> 00:08:04.150
Every one of their four-dimensional vectors, including 4-velocity, is zero at all times.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:08.721
You can see how this doesn't really feel like a speed, even though it's defined the same way.

00:08:08.721 --> 00:08:13.292
So, do we all travel at the speed of light? Eh, kind of.

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:16.529
Our four-dimensional speed is the speed of light,

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:19.131
but that's only true for matter, not light.

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:22.802
And that's probably not what people think of when they hear the word “speed.”

00:08:22.802 --> 00:08:26.572
If a factoid sounds simple, it's probably at least a little wrong.

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:30.810
That’s why it’s so important to be diligent when you hear a new piece of information,

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.079
especially when dealing with current events.

